{
  "title": "Entities and value objects",
  "idea": "In domain-driven design (DDD), an entity is defined by a lasting identity, while a value object is defined only by its values and never changes.",
  "analogy": {
    "text": "You stay the same person after moving house; your passport number tracks you. A ten-euro note is any ten-euro note; nobody cares which.",
    "breaks": "Collectors do care about serial numbers; your domain decides what matters."
  },
  "points": [
    "Entity: equality and `hashCode` use the id only; other fields may change over time.",
    "Value object: immutable, validated in its constructor, equal when all its values are equal.",
    "Java `record` gives value objects field-based `equals` and `hashCode` for free.",
    "Wrap raw amounts and emails in value objects so their rules live once."
  ],
