  ],
  "pitfalls": [
    "Putting mutable fields into an entity's `hashCode` loses it inside a `HashSet` or `HashMap`.",
    "Setters on a value object cause aliasing bugs; return a new instance from each operation instead.",
    "Passing raw `long` ids and `String` emails everywhere repeats validation and invites mix-ups."
  ],
  "quiz": {
    "q": "What does this print? `Customer` uses only its id in `equals` and `hashCode`.",
    "code": "var ada = new Customer(new CustomerId(7), new Email(\"a@x.io\"));\nSet<Customer> s = new HashSet<>(Set.of(ada));\nada.changeEmail(new Email(\"b@x.io\"));\nSystem.out.println(s.contains(ada));",
    "lang": "java",
    "options": [
      "false",
      "throws",
      "true"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "why": "`hashCode` uses only the id, which never changes, so the set still looks in the same bucket and finds `ada`."
  },
  "takeaway": "Entities are equal by id; value objects by value, and never change.",
